A||N||12||Joe Trainer||Fiero safety||||1||
Z||000000||Joe Trainer||01-18-2000||11:56 PM||||I'm considering the purchase of a 88GT and was asked by family members - wife and son - how safe is a Fiero in a crash?  They heard horror stories from friends regarding fires and severe occupant injuries during accidents.  This is the only issue preventing the purchase of the car at this time.  Your help please.  Negative and positive comments are appreciated.  Thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||01-19-2000||12:08 AM||||Nearly as safe as a Volvo based on an independant study in the 80's. I'd say thats pretty darn good...Anyone have that saftey link ??? Buy it Joe , you'll love it !!!||||reg||14
Z||000002||Cooter||01-19-2000||12:14 AM||||During the years of production, the Fiero was as safe as many larger cars and is comparable to the same year model Volvo 240 sedan in resistance to occupant injuries in collisions. Only the 84's had the rare engine fire problem and it was due to owner stupidity in most cases- continued driving while the engine was overheating. The Fiero pioneered the "safety cage" construction that is so popular these days. I'm sure other members can post the links to crashed Fieros where the owners survived, despite having a badly tore up car, many with only minor injuries.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||01-19-2000||12:18 AM||||The guy with the crutches was <B>IN</B> this Fiero when he hit a tree at 80 kph! He now has another GT Fiero. I wouldn't advise trying this at home without adult supervision  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/inspect.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||01-19-2000||12:23 AM||||Looks like the Fiero is strong enough to not crush you if you can <B>possibly</B> flip it. I dunno how one can roll a car as wide as the Fiero, but I guess it's possible. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/crash5_4.jpg">  I think they're safe!||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||01-19-2000||01:41 AM||||anyone have the link for the accident where the frame snapped in half when the car hit a truck?<P>it was posted here less than 2 months ago||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||01-19-2000||01:45 AM||||this came from the Fiero mailing list, sorry if you already have this..<P>My daughter was a passenger in her Formula when her ex-boyfriend ran a light at about 45 mph downtown two years ago last Thanksgiving night.  A V-10 Dodge Ram pickup hit the side of the car so hard that it pushed it in far enough that it bruised my daughter's right hip.  The Fiero flew into the air and hit the side of the local newspaper building about 5 feet off the ground, breaking the granite siding and causing $1500 worth of damage<BR>to the building.  It spun around 180 degrees and landed hard on its wheels. The right side where it was hit was pushed in a foot and a half.  The drivers side that hit the building was scraped hard from headlight to tail<BR>light.  The Front fascia was destroyed, I don't know how.  The Dodge Ram fired it's air bag and between that and the sheet metal and suspension damage it sustained over $6000 in damages. It was towed away.  My daughter and her stupid boyfriend walked away from the accident. Of course neither one was wearing a seat belt.  After all,teenagers are notoriously bullet proof anyway. My hardheaded daughter refused to let the paramedics take her to the hospital and stayed the night with a friend after calling home and neglecting to tell me she had been in an accident that destroyed her car.<BR>She told me the next day and a friend drove the Fiero onto a trailer (yeah, it sort of crab walked since it was pushed in in the middle but it started and ran...the coolant tube was of course broken so it didn't have much antifreeze if any but it ran well enough to go onto the trailer) and we backed it off the trailer and drove it up the drive and into the garage when he brought it home for us.<P>I thought it was a total loss for sure and I gave it to a roofing crew member who re roofed my house the following spring so he could salvage the engine.  I held the title until he brought back the lace wheels and 4 new tires which was our agreement.<P>He somehow got it in driving order and used it to rob a bank the following July. He apparently got a duplicate registration and plates and it was quite a surprise when the police called and asked about the bank robbery after tracing the license plates to my daughter.  Luckily they already had<BR>located the car and had a suspect, but were curious as to why he had my daughter's car.  The city impounded it of course and I could have had it back since she still was the registered owner, but the cost of the towing,<BR>impound fees, storage charges etc. were worth more than 4 lace wheels and tires so the City of Des Moines disposed of it themselves somehow.<P>some interesting stuff...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||01-19-2000||05:57 PM||||The <B> only </B> car to beat the Fiero in NHTSA crash tests (while the Fiero was in production) was the Volvo!!!!  The Volvo has ranked in the top 5 every year!!  I guess that tells me that the Fiero is pretty safe.  I'll take it over anything else any day!||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||01-19-2000||06:13 PM||||hey, anybody know the status of the trany in the wrecked GT?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>of course, it's my duty to ask such questions!||||reg||10
Z||000009||DRH||01-19-2000||06:31 PM||||I have yet to hear of a negative safety comment about a Fiero that could be backed up. I have heard plenty of positive ones and quite a few had pictures. The guy on crutches above is one of the best examples. <P>My stepson was recently t-boned in the passenger side of an 84 Fiero by a Cadillac Cimmaron doing 30+ MPH. Even with no seatbelt, he was relatively unhurt.<P>My opinion is that Fieros are safer than a lot of cars on the road (including some much newer ones).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||01-20-2000||07:26 PM||||An excellent and well publicised example of Fiero crashworthyness was ina Readers Digest "Drama in Real Life" feature a few years ago. A girl in Texas I believe was waiting at a train crossing when her car was hit from behind with enough force to push it through the gate and into the path of an oncoming Amtrak. She and her car were dragged 150 YARDS(!!) before the back end struck a pole and whipped the car from under the train. She survived with some moderate injuries. And no, the car did not catch on fire.||||reg||1
Z||000011||86SE||01-20-2000||07:36 PM||||read the page on safety on my web page, there's a link on the left side colum.<P> <A HREF="http://fierodude.hypermart.net" TARGET=_blank>http://fierodude.hypermart.net</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||ka4nkf||01-20-2000||07:48 PM||||I like my fiero. All race cars are pretty save because they have a roll frame built inside. The fiero is safe because it has a space frame around it. the doors and hood, fenders ,trunk are all jus bolted to this frame. You just dont get any better than this. Get the fiero you will love it.||||reg||1
